WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Sometimes a maths puzzle tells you the finish, but hides the

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.700
start. Working backwards means beginning with that finish and

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.540
undoing the moves.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.260
There is one crucial rule: undo the last move first.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.240
Like taking off your shoes before your socks, the order

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:14.540
matters.

00:00:15.496 --> 00:00:18.056
This is useful when guessing the starting number would take

00:00:18.056 --> 00:00:21.796
ages. If each move can be undone, we can follow a

00:00:21.796 --> 00:00:23.156
clear route back to the start.

00:00:23.876 --> 00:00:26.256
Then we check the answer by travelling forwards again.

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:28.219
Let's try a number machine.

00:00:28.819 --> 00:00:31.739
It multiplies a secret number by four, then adds three.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:33.679
The machine finishes at twenty-three.

00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:35.179
What number went in?

00:00:35.599 --> 00:00:37.919
Notice the order: multiply first, then add.

00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:39.527
Start at twenty-three.

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.147
The last thing the machine did was add three, so undo

00:00:43.147 --> 00:00:46.727
that first. Twenty-three minus three is twenty.

00:00:46.727 --> 00:00:49.147
We have recovered the amount just before the final addition.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.750
Only now do we undo the multiplication.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.350
Twenty divided by four is five.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.390
Check the whole journey: five times four is twenty, and twenty

00:00:58.390 --> 00:00:59.610
plus three is twenty-three.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:00.950
Five works.

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:03.985
What if we undo the operations in the wrong order?

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:08.405
Twenty-three divided by four is five point seven five.

00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:11.865
Subtract three, and we get two point seven five.

00:01:11.865 --> 00:01:12.825
That is not five.

00:01:13.285 --> 00:01:14.625
The order is part of the method.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:17.935
Here is why the correct order works for any starting number.

00:01:18.575 --> 00:01:19.155
Call the start x.

00:01:19.835 --> 00:01:21.755
The machine makes four x plus three.

00:01:22.335 --> 00:01:24.675
Subtracting three leaves four x.

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:26.155
Dividing by four leaves x.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:28.995
We peel away the outside operation first.

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:32.935
Multiplying by zero would lose the starting information, so

00:01:32.935 --> 00:01:34.655
that move cannot be undone uniquely.

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:37.005
Now try a different kind of puzzle.

00:01:37.705 --> 00:01:39.645
A child keeps half of a collection of counters.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:41.985
Then they keep two thirds of what remains.

00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:44.205
At the end, there are eight counters.

00:01:45.025 --> 00:01:46.225
How many were there at the start?

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:48.199
Work backwards from eight.

00:01:48.999 --> 00:01:51.399
These eight counters are two thirds of the previous pile.

00:01:51.839 --> 00:01:54.079
Split them into two equal groups of four.

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:57.039
Restore the missing third, another group of four.

00:01:57.719 --> 00:01:59.419
The previous pile had twelve counters.

00:02:00.362 --> 00:02:02.322
Twelve was half of the original collection.

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.202
Double it to recover twenty-four.

00:02:05.062 --> 00:02:05.822
Now check forwards.

00:02:06.582 --> 00:02:07.742
Half of twenty-four is twelve.

00:02:08.482 --> 00:02:09.702
Two thirds of twelve is eight.

00:02:10.122 --> 00:02:10.942
Every stage agrees.

00:02:11.817 --> 00:02:14.657
The common trap is using the wrong whole.

00:02:14.657 --> 00:02:17.797
Two thirds of what remains means two thirds of twelve, not

00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:19.557
two thirds of the original twenty-four.

00:02:20.537 --> 00:02:23.437
Write the amount after every stage so the whole stays clear.

00:02:24.004 --> 00:02:26.564
There is one more check when a puzzle uses whole counters.

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:29.604
Every intermediate pile must also be whole.

00:02:30.284 --> 00:02:33.744
Suppose a rule halves the pile, adds one counter, then doubles

00:02:33.744 --> 00:02:36.464
it. Could the final pile contain seven?

00:02:37.112 --> 00:02:37.832
Undo the steps.

00:02:38.672 --> 00:02:40.972
Seven divided by two is three point five.

00:02:41.512 --> 00:02:43.592
Subtract one to get two point five.

00:02:44.152 --> 00:02:45.032
Double it to get five.

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:48.472
The starting number is whole, but the intermediate piles are

00:02:48.472 --> 00:02:51.092
not. You cannot have half a counter in this puzzle.

00:02:51.716 --> 00:02:53.676
So seven is impossible under these rules.

00:02:54.436 --> 00:02:55.256
Try eight instead.

00:02:55.836 --> 00:02:58.476
Backwards, eight becomes four, then three, then six.

00:02:58.476 --> 00:03:02.036
Forwards, six becomes three, then four, then eight.

00:03:02.796 --> 00:03:04.876
Every pile is whole, and every move is legal.

00:03:05.594 --> 00:03:06.494
Remember the method.

00:03:07.174 --> 00:03:07.714
Reverse the order.

00:03:08.214 --> 00:03:09.134
Undo each operation.

00:03:10.054 --> 00:03:11.374
Check every intermediate state.

00:03:12.034 --> 00:03:13.914
Then check the complete forward journey.

00:03:14.574 --> 00:03:17.734
A backward calculation finds a candidate; the checks show

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:18.814
whether it really works.

00:03:19.556 --> 00:03:23.236
Your turn. A number is multiplied by three, then four is

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:25.576
subtracted. The result is seventeen.

00:03:26.376 --> 00:03:29.576
Pause here. Recover the starting number, then draw the forward

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:30.856
journey to prove your answer.

